    "q-317541": {
      "dom_id": "step-q317541",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-2({3}^{x})+1?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=2({3}^{x}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/7a11b33423337a8d7f0222e1586753d62374f3d8b3eca02cccadd99e50b9eb1b.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-2({3}^{x}).$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/53aedebd12a8f1138efcc9e9815dba7cf15d869da4f321274f6b0cd367e28cac.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit up to give $y=-2({3}^{x})+1.$ The point $(0,-2)$ moves to $(0,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/d862abef96431d8165d0b6c1fd76419597f828824fab1bf88eb4ccba5e6dfda8.png)",
